-- title:
--   Inertia-invariant forms kill points reducing to the identity
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $O$ be a commutative ring equipped with an algebra map to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ in the sense that the image of $p$ is a non-unit of $P$; assume every element of the image of $O$ lies in $P$ and also in a fixed intermediate field $F$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$ that is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $H$ be a $p$-divisible group over $O$ of height $h$, that is, a family of commutative cocommutative $O$-Hopf algebras `H.level v`, finite and free over $O$ of rank $p^{vh}$, together with surjective coalgebra maps `H.transition v` from level $v+1$ to level $v$ whose kernels are the ideals generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^{v}$. Write $T$ for the Tate module of the group of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $H$, i.e. the group of sequences $(z_n)$ of points with $p^{n}z_n = 0$ and $p\,z_{n+1} = z_n$, where the points of level $v$ are the $O$-algebra maps `H.level v` $\to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with convolution group law and `H.Points` is the direct limit over $v$. Let $f : T \to \mathbb{Z}_p$ be $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear and assume: for every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism $\tau$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ belonging to the inertia subgroup of $P$ inside $\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ (the image of the inertia subgroup of the decomposition subgroup) and every $O$-algebra automorphism $\tau'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ agreeing with $\tau$ at every element, $f$ is invariant under the termwise action of $\tau'$ on $T$. Let $x \in T$ be such that for every $n$ there are a level $w$ and a point $g$ of level $w$ with $g$, viewed in `H.Points`, equal to the $n$-th term of $x$, and with $P$-valuation of $g(a) - \varepsilon(a)$ (the image under $O \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the counit of $a$ subtracted from the value of the algebra map attached to $g$) strictly less than $1$ for all $a \in$ `H.level w`. Then $f(x) = 0$.
--
--   This is the global form of Tate's statement that the kernel of reduction in the Tate module of a $p$-divisible group at a place above $p$ is annihilated by every inertia-invariant $\mathbb{Z}_p$-valued functional, i.e. lies in the part of the Tate module seen by the connected component. It is used in the analysis of the Tate modules attached to the modular curve $X_1(p)$ and of the pairings occurring there.
